WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:16.770
In a 1677 letter to the Royal Society of London,
the Dutch tradesman and microbiology pioneer

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:22.829
Antony van Leeuwenhoek described the strange
appendages he had found on a creature in a

00:00:22.829 --> 00:00:25.060
sample of rainwater.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:33.100
He observed that the organism was "provided
with divers incredibly thin little feet, or

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.980
little legs, which were moved very nimbly."

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:42.219
We don't know exactly what organism he was
describing, just that it was some sort of

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:44.579
ciliated protozoan.

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:50.839
But this letter is important because the "little
legs" he was describing, constitute one of

00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:56.860
the earliest descriptions of a cellular organelle.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.550
Leeuwenhoek was referring to the cilia.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:06.130
That name would be bestowed in 1786 by Otto
Muller, who took inspiration from the word

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.580
"cilium," which translates to "eyelash" in
Latin.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:15.760
Today, we know that cilia are a bundle of
microtubules, and that the synchronized beating

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.460
of motile cilia can create currents in the
water.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:26.230
We can often see cilia in action, waving around
along the edges of a single-celled eukaryote

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:31.890
as they move around, or even crowning more
complex organisms like rotifers to help them

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.570
gather food.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.140
So sometimes cilia are a way to move around.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.660
And sometimes they're a way to eat.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.390
And sometimes they're both!

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:48.000
But sometimes, they let an organism lead the
most ideal lifestyle of all: one where you

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.860
just hang out and let the food come to you.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:59.650
One group of ciliates that manages to strive
toward that ideal are the peritrich ciliates,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.700
a diverse group of protists that contain around
1,000 species.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.890
That number making up about 1/7 of all ciliates
we currently know about.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:16.599
Peritrich ciliates are characterized by their
vase-like, or depending on where you’re

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:24.150
from vase-like shape, which opens into an
enlarged mouth-like structure called the peristome

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.040
that is lined with--what else?--cilia.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:32.920
One of the most well-studied peritrich ciliates
are vorticella, whose cilia you can see waving

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.469
around here.

00:02:34.469 --> 00:02:39.599
Their peristomes are lined with two bands
of cilia: one to generate water currents and

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:45.489
to draw food towards their mouth-y thing,
and another to filter through the particles.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:50.540
And it turns out that all that movement is
quite effective.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:57.060
Scientists measuring some vorticella found
that even when their cilia are only 17 micrometers

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:05.069
long, they can bring in particles that were
450 micrometers away.

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:11.400
Imagine if there were a delicious snack 50
feet away from you, and all you had to do

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.230
was wave your arms in the air.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:18.469
Vorticella: truly living the dream.

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:23.849
We featured Vorticella on a recent episode,
where we followed them in real time as they

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:29.530
dealt with rude neighbors and other little
microcosmic things.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:35.150
We were able to film that episode because
Vorticella belong to the larger order of peritrich

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:41.810
ciliates called the Sessilida, which--as the
name suggests--are all sessile.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.749
That means for most of their life, sessilid
peritrich ciliates are found attached to other

00:03:47.749 --> 00:03:52.099
surfaces, be they rock, plant, or something
else.

00:03:52.099 --> 00:04:00.480
In contrast, the rest of the peritrich ciliates are grouped together as members of the free-swimming Mobilida.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.890
Of course, while these distinctions are useful,
things are not necessarily as neat as "these

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.430
organisms swim" and "these organisms don't."

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:14.829
Sessilid peritrichs live their lives in one
of two distinct forms.

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:20.919
The dominant one--the one that defines them--is
their adult, sessile form, which is called

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:22.460
the trophont stage.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:28.870
The trophont sessilid is attached to a surface,
like the vorticella using its long stalk to

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.690
stake its place.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:36.949
And its body lacks any cilia except for those
distinct bands around their peristome.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:43.690
But in their youth, sessilid peritrichs are
free-swimmers called telotrochs.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:50.370
In contrast to their trophont stages, these
telotrochs usually don't feed, so their oral

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.710
cilia are tucked away.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:59.210
But they do have a single band of cilia around
their bodies called the trochal band, which

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:04.580
helps them swim around until they find something
to attach to.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:10.909
While the teletroch is generally associated
with young sessilid peritrichs, a trophont

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:17.470
can return to this stage if it needs to find
a new home, like this Campanella that’s

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:31.080
decided to leave its stalk and swim off somewhere
new.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:37.159
And just like sessilid peritrichs do sometimes
move around, there are so-called Mobilid peritrichs

00:05:37.159 --> 00:05:41.860
with an adhesive disc that allows them to
attach to things.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:48.970
But unlike Sessilids, mobilids never lose
their trochal cilia that help them swim around.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:54.069
And when they swim around, they really do
put the “mobile” in “mobilid”...we

00:05:54.069 --> 00:05:56.770
can barely keep this one in the frame!

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:01.430
The evolutionary lines connecting these two
orders are vague.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:07.689
There have been hypotheses that mobilid peritrichs
evolved from the swimming telotroch stage

00:06:07.689 --> 00:06:09.740
of the sessilid life cycle.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:14.710
But attempts to use molecular phylogeny to
verify that prediction at a genetic level

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.430
have come up with varied results.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:24.580
So there's a chance that these different organisms
are related, but they also may be two unrelated

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:30.930
groups of organisms who stumbled on the same
ciliated oral structure through convergent

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.060
evolution.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:39.479
Whichever order they belong to, the peritrich
ciliates that do attach to a substrate make

00:06:39.479 --> 00:06:41.370
for an interesting study.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:49.400
Part of it is the way they bring their own
drama to what seems like a dull, sedentary life.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:54.639
You might have noticed this in our real time
episode, where the vorticella rapidly contracted

00:06:54.639 --> 00:06:57.120
in response to passing organisms.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:04.360
When this happens, a vorticella’s stalk
can collapse to around 20-40% of its original

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:11.520
length in milliseconds, a fast movement that
is followed eventually by a much slower and

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.159
more chill relaxation back to full length.

00:07:16.159 --> 00:07:26.509
In his own observation of Vorticella, Leeuwenhoek
noted that he found this sight "mightily diverting."

00:07:26.509 --> 00:07:35.810
But the choices that peritrich ciliates make
in where to set up their stalks can be “mightily

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:38.300
diverting” as well.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:42.569
Sometimes it's the typical substrates, things
like plants or rocks.

00:07:42.569 --> 00:07:45.800
But sometimes, it's animals.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.360
Sometimes these interactions can become more
fraught.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.189
Mobilid peritrichs, for example, are known
to infect fishes and amphibians, creating

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:58.290
both ecological and economic challenges.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:04.499
But when these interactions aren’t harmful,
you get something called epibiosis, where

00:08:04.499 --> 00:08:09.479
an organism lives on another organism without
being parasitic.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:14.479
And judging by the many varied animal homes
that peritrich ciliates have chosen, they’re

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:20.039
not particularly picky, like these ciliates
that have found a place on this water flea

00:08:20.039 --> 00:08:22.080
to cozy up to.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.469
Some have been found in the gills of blue
crabs.

00:08:25.469 --> 00:08:27.210
Others have been found on tardigrades.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.729
And snails.

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:30.099
And pelagic diatoms.

00:08:30.099 --> 00:08:33.909
And the antenna of European crayfish.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:38.800
The list truly goes on.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:45.180
In a way that has delighted us for centuries,
using those tiny little legs in their mouths

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:51.740
and along their bodies, peritrich ciliates
have managed to carve out a very specific--and

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:59.770
some might say ideal--way of life that seems
to have helped them survive just about anywhere.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:05.880
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

